-- Source module: Solutions.KFDBase
/-!
# KFD base: fine compilation of register programs

The register programs `RS` of KIL1–KIL6 are compiled by `RS.code`, whose counted loop
`rep i n b` keeps a *binary* counter on `i` and decrements it in every iteration (cost
`Θ(zsz n)` per iteration).  `fcode` compiles the same program with a *unary* counter instead:

* `rep i n b ↦ push i n; toUL i i; loop i isMk (unmark i; fcode b); drop i`

(the value `n` stays below the `n` marks as a sentinel; `toUL` is the linear binary → unary
conversion of `KFCUnary`).  Everything else is compiled as by `RS.code`.

`fcode_runs` (**simulation**): if `p.ok s` and every loop body leaves its counter alone
(`FOK p`: the counter is not *touched* — read or written — by the body, and avoids the
`toUL` scratch stacks `26 … 29`), then `fcode p` realises `p.run` with the cost `fcost p s`.
The run is proved relative to a set of *ghost* stacks (the counters of the enclosing loops,
which hold marks in the fine run and the binary count in the `RS` semantics).

`fcost_le_cost`: `fcost p s ≤ 100 · p.cost s` (coarse transfer of every `KIL` cost bound).
The point of `fcost` is the loop: per iteration it charges `fcost b + 31` plus the amortised
`toUL` cost, i.e. `O(1)` overhead per iteration, independent of the size of the count.

`FSp p s t c` is the fine analogue of `KIL.Sp`.
-/

